WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Deployment and Implementation Success Factors

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Welcome to this session on deployment planning.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.566
Specifically, how do we deploy and implement Six Sigma.

4
00:00:12.566 --> 00:00:14.866
To begin the session, I want to look at

5
00:00:14.866 --> 00:00:18.566
and scrutinize some of the success factors.

6
00:00:18.566 --> 00:00:22.499
That will help us indirectly understand how to better

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.666
implement and deploy. Specifically, how do we plan deployments?

8
00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:30.199
Well, recall what we said in the past,

9
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.966
that in order to successfully deploy it,

10
00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:39.232
we have to have clearly defined business related needs.

11
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:43.199
If those business needs are not clearly defined,

12
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:49.100
it's going to be very difficult to define good deployment plans.

13
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:55.466
Then we have to translate business and customer needs into metrics.

14
00:00:55.466 --> 00:00:59.766
And that's the key here. Absolute key.

15
00:00:59.766 --> 00:01:06.332
Is the ability to translate customer needs into metrics.

16
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:12.533
Third, we must be resolved to achieve true,

17
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:19.499
true world-class status. We’ve gotta have the will to do that.

18
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.533
Fourth, we need superordinate goals based on benchmarking.

19
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:29.533
In other words, we can't just say we're best in class.

20
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:34.799
We need to have quantitative goals based on benchmarking

21
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.066
and then set superordinate levels of change, of improvement.

22
00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:44.799
Absolutely essential.

23
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.233
I want to focus on that word superordinate.

24
00:01:49.233 --> 00:01:56.166
It’s a very keyword. Stretch goals.

25
00:01:56.166 --> 00:02:01.066
Superordinate. Because we want people to re-examine the way in which the work get done,

26
00:02:01.066 --> 00:02:06.332
not tweak the existing system. So, we set superordinate goals based on benchmarking.

27
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:11.899
Then we want and uncompromising roadmap for achieving breakthrough.

28
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.866
Now, why do I say uncompromising? We don't want to see

29
00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:22.366
it watered down over time. So, it's got to be uncompromising.

30
00:02:22.366 --> 00:02:29.299
Six. We want the assignment of our best people to the Six Sigma roles.

31
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:35.400
Absolute must. Define a career path to these Six Sigma roles.

32
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.400
A defined career path. Now, that I don't mean you’re going to be a career black belt.

33
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.400
What that means is you leave a job, come in to become a black belt,

34
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.866
and you go on into another job of higher responsibility.

35
00:02:48.866 --> 00:02:54.499
So people that participate as a black belt want a defined career path,

36
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.500
once they have succeeded.

37
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.400
That's in line with the idea of meaningful rewards and recognition

38
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.833
for all the Six Sigma players.

39
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:10.833
We want vertically correlated performance metrics. Absolutely essential.

40
00:03:10.833 --> 00:03:15.833
Our business metrics must be vertically correlated to the operations metrics

41
00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:18.799
and to the process metrics.

42
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:24.966
We want variable pay linked to performance of those metrics. That's item number 10.

43
00:03:24.966 --> 00:03:29.932
Linking pay is key to performance.

44
00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:34.499
Then we want nonnegotiable performance targets for those projects.

45
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.966
Absolutely nonnegotiable targets for black belt projects.

46
00:03:39.966 --> 00:03:45.632
We want documented project selection process. That’s another key to success.

47
00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:50.766
You've gotta have a process that’s documented on how you're going to select

48
00:03:50.766 --> 00:03:57.599
your application projects. Not just for black belts, but green belts and white belts as well.

49
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.600
We’ve also found that you gotta have systems to support, track, and report progress.

50
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:08.166
Without that, you don't know where you're going.

51
00:04:08.166 --> 00:04:12.166
It’s like trying to steer the ship by looking at the wake of the boat.

52
00:04:12.166 --> 00:04:17.366
So you gotta be able to have those systems, like radar, to help you track

53
00:04:17.366 --> 00:04:20.366
and report your progress.

54
00:04:20.366 --> 00:04:25.366
You gotta commit the economic resources. Without that, you're going nowhere.

55
00:04:25.366 --> 00:04:29.932
Get rid of all the alphabet soup in your organization.

56
00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:35.799
Get rid of the UFO, KGB, DDY, ABC programs,

57
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.333
and soft initiatives that are not linked.

58
00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:46.766
See, to your focus. Get rid of those.

59
00:04:46.766 --> 00:04:53.732
Have an intolerance to pilot programs and proof of principle projects.

60
00:04:53.733 --> 00:04:58.799
And finally, self-sustain your training and education.

61
00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.400
Last thing you want is a whole bunch of consultants in your organization,

62
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.433
you can't get rid of for sixteen years.

63
00:05:04.433 --> 00:05:07.466
You’ve gotta be self-sustaining.

64
00:05:07.466 --> 00:05:13.466
Create the master black belts and everything to self-sustain your initiative.

65
00:05:13.466 --> 00:05:17.199
Finally, people love to hear success. Tell them.

66
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.233
Get out there and create a communications effort to communicate

67
00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:26.233
your Six Sigma successes. That's absolutely essential.

68
00:05:26.233 --> 00:05:32.266
So those are some of the critical lessons learned.

69
00:05:32.266 --> 00:05:36.366
For me personally, after about 20 years and  quite a few deployments.

70
00:05:36.366 --> 00:05:41.266
Probably on the order forty, fifty major corporations of deploying this stuff in.

71
00:05:41.266 --> 00:05:46.566
Of what I’ve walked away with, an understanding of what constitutes success.

72
00:05:46.566 --> 00:05:51.666
What I want to make sure that happens and does not happen.

73
00:05:51.666 --> 00:05:58.532
So again summarizing. We gotta have defined needs; turn needs into metrics;

74
00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:01.633
set true world-class goals,  superordinate goals;

75
00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:04.666
have an uncompromising roadmap on how we are going to get there;

76
00:06:04.666 --> 00:06:09.466
assign our best people; define career paths for what's going to happen to these players

77
00:06:09.466 --> 00:06:14.666
after they go back to their jobs; have meaningful rewards for their success;

78
00:06:14.666 --> 00:06:17.766
ensure that performance metrics are vertically correlated;

79
00:06:17.766 --> 00:06:20.766
have your pay linked to those metrics;

80
00:06:20.766 --> 00:06:24.066
have non-negotiable targets for projects;

81
00:06:24.066 --> 00:06:27.432
systems to support the selection of projects;

82
00:06:27.433 --> 00:06:31.899
a process to select those projects; commit the economic resources

83
00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.600
to make Six Sigma happen; get rid of all your alphabet soup stuff;

84
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.133
have an intolerance to pilot programs and proof of principle;

85
00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:44.133
have self-sustained training; and communicate your successes.

86
00:06:44.133 --> 00:06:48.133
You do those things in a high-quality way

87
00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:54.033
and you're going to be well on your way to having a good Six Sigma deployment.

88
00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:59.033
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
